INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY NOTICE

The concept, design, architecture, and full implementation of this
platform — including but not limited to:

  • The integration of NFC (Near Field Communication) card technology
    with restaurant QR menu systems for contactless ordering;
  • The workflow of writing QR codes onto NFC cards and distributing
    them as physical touchpoints for digital menus;
  • The combined use of NFC card ordering + QR digital menu +
    multi-tenant business management + POS system within a single
    SaaS platform;

— are original works conceived, designed, and developed solely by
Deniz Sevinç, and are protected under applicable intellectual
property and copyright law.

ORIGINAL SECURITY FINDING — PROTECTED INTELLECTUAL DISCOVERY

In the course of developing this platform, the author independently
identified a critical and previously unaddressed security
vulnerability in QR-based restaurant ordering systems:

  VULNERABILITY: A QR code used for restaurant table ordering is
  inherently insecure. Because a QR code is a static, copyable URL,
  any person who photographs or scans the code — even after leaving
  the premises — can submit fraudulent orders to the restaurant
  system indefinitely, from any location, without physical presence.
  This constitutes a "fake order" or "remote order spoofing" attack
  vector that requires zero technical knowledge to exploit.

  SOLUTION CONCEIVED BY THE AUTHOR: By embedding the QR code link
  into a programmable NFC card and using the NFC card as the sole
  order interface, the author eliminated this attack vector. NFC
  requires physical card proximity (typically ≤4 cm) to activate.
  A card cannot be copied via photograph, screenshot, or remote
  means. This hardware-enforced presence requirement ensures that
  orders can only be placed by someone physically at the table,
  with the card in hand.

  TOKEN-BASED SESSION ARCHITECTURE (original design):
  Every order session initiated — whether via QR or NFC — transmits
  a time-limited session token to the system. The token validity
  duration is configurable by the business owner. The distinction
  is critical:

    - With QR: the code can be scanned repeatedly from any location,
      and a new token is issued on each scan. A copied QR link can
      therefore obtain valid tokens indefinitely without restriction.

    - With NFC: a token can only be issued upon physical tap of the
      NFC card (proximity ≤4 cm). If the card is absent, remote,
      or in unauthorized hands, no token is issued and no order
      session can be initiated.

  This creates a hardware-enforced access gate: no physical card =
  no token = no order. The architecture prevents both fake order
  injection and token hijacking at the hardware level.

This security analysis, the identification of the vulnerability,
and the architectural decision to solve it via NFC are original
intellectual contributions by Deniz Sevinç. Any product or service
that adopts this specific security rationale — using NFC as a
replacement for QR in restaurant ordering to prevent fake/remote
order attacks — without attribution or prior written consent from
the author may constitute intellectual property infringement.

